{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both acceptance lines:\n- loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\n- npm test passes\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Right now loadConfig is `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`. src/app.js wraps it in try/catch and prints `bad config: <message>` then exits 1. README.md documents that behaviour.\n2. In src/config.js, read the file with readFileSync outside the try block, so read errors such as a missing file still throw as before. Wrap only the JSON.parse call in try/catch. In the catch, return {}. Only catch the parse error (a SyntaxError); any other error should be rethrown. Valid JSON must still be returned parsed, unchanged.\n3. Update test/config.test.js. The existing test 'bad JSON throws' expects a SyntaxError, which goes against the new behaviour. Replace it with a test that loadConfig on invalid JSON (for example `{port: 9000`) deep-equals {}. Keep the 'reads a config' test.\n4. README.md says invalid JSON stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1. Since loadConfig no longer throws on bad JSON, update that sentence to describe the new behaviour: the app starts with its defaults. Leave the catch in src/app.js alone, because read errors still reach it.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure every test passes.\n6. Open the pull request. In its description, note that returning {} hides the parse error: the user is not told which file is broken or where. Optionally, before returning {}, write a one-line warning to stderr that names the path and the parse error message. That does not change the return value.","verifier":"Run these checks at the PR head, from the repository root. Run `npm install` first only if package.json now lists dependencies.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun: write a file containing invalid JSON, for example `printf '{port: 9000' > /tmp/bad-config.json`. Then run `node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig('/tmp/bad-config.json'); console.log(JSON.stringify(r));\"`.\nPASS: the command does not throw, and the printed value is `{}` (an object with no keys).\nFAIL: loadConfig throws (a SyntaxError or anything else escapes), or it returns something that is not an empty object, or src/config.js no longer exports loadConfig(path).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test` from the repository root.\nPASS: the test run reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: any test fails or errors, or the test run itself errors.\n\nAdvisory (not PASS/FAIL conditions):\n- Check that valid JSON still parses: loadConfig on a file containing `{\"port\": 9000}` should return { port: 9000 }.\n- Check that a missing file still throws (ENOENT) rather than returning {}, since the line covers only the JSON.parse error.\n- Check that the old 'bad JSON throws' test was replaced, not just deleted, by a test that bad JSON returns {}.\n- Check that README.md no longer claims invalid JSON exits 1 with `bad config:`.\n- Note whether a warning naming the file and the parse error is printed. Without one, the user still can't tell which file is broken. Report this as an observation only."}